    {
      "@id": "concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-53",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "53 Data profiling to track online behaviour is prohibited",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/digital-id-act-2024#sec-53"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "digital-id-act-2024.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "digital-id-act-2024.pdf, p.68",
      "values:originalText": "(1) An accredited entity must not use or disclose information if:\n(a) the information is personal information about an individual\nthat is in the entity’s possession or control; and\n(b) the information is any of the following:\n(i) information about the services provided by the entity\nthat the individual has accessed, or attempted to access;\n(ii) information relating to how or when access was\nobtained or attempted to be obtained by the individual;\n(iii) information relating to the method of access or\nattempted access by the individual;\n(iv) the date and time the individual’s identity was verified.\n\nPrivacy  Chapter 3\nPrivacy  Part 2\nAdditional privacy safeguards  Division 2\nSection 54\nDigital ID Act 2024\nCompilation date: 21/02/2025\nCivil penalty:\n1,500 penalty units.\n(2) Subsection (1) applies even if the individual has consented to the\nuse or disclosure.\n(3) However, subsection (1) does not apply if the use or disclosure:\n(a) is for purposes relating to improving the performance or\nuseability of the entity’s information technology system\nthrough which the entity’s accredited services are provided\nand not for broader business purposes; or\n(b) is for the purposes of the entity complying with this Act; or\n(c) is required or authorised by or under a law of the\nCommonwealth, a State or a Territory.\nNote:\nA person who wishes to rely on this subsection bears an evidential\nburden in relation to the matter mentioned in this subsection (see\nsection 96 of the Regulatory Powers Act).",
      "skos:definition": "(1) An accredited entity must not use or disclose information if:\n(a) the information is personal information about an individual\nthat is in the entity’s possession or control; and\n(b) the information is any of the following:\n(i) information about the services provided by the entity\nthat the individual has accessed, or attempted to access;\n(ii) information relating to how or when access was\nobtained or attempted to be obtained by the individual;\n(iii) information relating to the method of access or\nattempted access by the individual;\n(iv) the date and time the individual’s identity was verified.\n\nPrivacy  Chapter 3\nPrivacy  Part 2\nAdditional privacy safeguards  Division 2\nSection 54\nDigital ID Act 2024\nCompilation date: 21/02/2025\nCivil penalty:\n1,500 penalty units.\n(2) Subsection (1) applies even if the individual has consented to the\nuse or disclosure.\n(3) However, subsection (1) does not apply if the use or disclosure:\n(a) is for purposes relating to improving the performance or\nuseability of the entity’s information technology system\nthrough which the entity’s accredited services are provided\nand not for broader business purposes; or\n(b) is for the purposes of the entity complying with this Act; or\n(c) is required or authorised by or under a law of the\nCommonwealth, a State or a Territory.\nNote:\nA person who wishes to rely on this subsection bears an evidential\nburden in relation to the matter mentioned in this subsection (see\nsection 96 of the Regulatory Powers Act).",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-53-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-53-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-53-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "109cfd2ff7a5448b25c5b6e207d88aaa5ad9e0d955c2699e146e2ab74dab5bd5"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/digital-id-act-2024#sec-53",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "53 Data profiling to track online behaviour is prohibited",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 68,
      "values:originalText": "(1) An accredited entity must not use or disclose information if:\n(a) the information is personal information about an individual\nthat is in the entity’s possession or control; and\n(b) the information is any of the following:\n(i) information about the services provided by the entity\nthat the individual has accessed, or attempted to access;\n(ii) information relating to how or when access was\nobtained or attempted to be obtained by the individual;\n(iii) information relating to the method of access or\nattempted access by the individual;\n(iv) the date and time the individual’s identity was verified.\n\nPrivacy  Chapter 3\nPrivacy  Part 2\nAdditional privacy safeguards  Division 2\nSection 54\nDigital ID Act 2024\nCompilation date: 21/02/2025\nCivil penalty:\n1,500 penalty units.\n(2) Subsection (1) applies even if the individual has consented to the\nuse or disclosure.\n(3) However, subsection (1) does not apply if the use or disclosure:\n(a) is for purposes relating to improving the performance or\nuseability of the entity’s information technology system\nthrough which the entity’s accredited services are provided\nand not for broader business purposes; or\n(b) is for the purposes of the entity complying with this Act; or\n(c) is required or authorised by or under a law of the\nCommonwealth, a State or a Territory.\nNote:\nA person who wishes to rely on this subsection bears an evidential\nburden in relation to the matter mentioned in this subsection (see\nsection 96 of the Regulatory Powers Act)."
    },
